00:00:20.295 --> 00:00:21.450
Good morning, folks.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.801
OK, adjust that for your
time of day, I suppose.

00:00:23.801 --> 00:00:24.300
Hi.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.200
I'm Tom Melendez.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.390
And this is next in a
series of short videos

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.680
showing you how you can use
Ansible with Google Cloud

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:30.690
Platform.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.880
If you saw our first
couple of videos,

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.530
you'll recall the
theme of these videos

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.240
is that I'm a DevOps
engineer using

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.030
Ansible to manage infrastructure
for our satellite imagery

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.440
project.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.880
Earlier, I received
a text letting

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.950
me know about an unexpected
traffic surge on our imagery

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:45.750
website.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.300
But we're autoscaling with
Google Cloud Platform,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.400
so I could keep playing my game.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.170
Dylan Silva is here
from Red Hat again,

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.420
joining me to talk about
how we're using Ansible

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.675
to manage our infrastructure.

00:00:55.675 --> 00:00:56.550
DYLAN SILVA: Hi, all.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.740
Tom, thanks for having me again.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.916
Can you describe the setup?

00:00:59.916 --> 00:01:01.290
And then I'll be
happy to walk us

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.250
through the Ansible playbooks,
like we did the last time.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:05.310
TOM MELENDEZ: Sure.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:06.900
Google Compute
Engine not only has

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.030
VMs, like we saw
in the last video,

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.280
but it also has lots of
networking capabilities.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.145
In this video, we'll
walk through how

00:01:14.145 --> 00:01:17.280
we're using GCE's
HTTP load balancer

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.620
to create a globally
accessible, cross-region,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.380
auto-scaling website
running on managed VMs,

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.802
all provisioned
with the Ansible.

00:01:24.802 --> 00:01:26.760
I'm going to kick off
the provisioning process,

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.620
and then hand it over
to Dylan to walk us

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.600
through these Ansible playbooks.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.150
You can watch our first
video if you need tips

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.522
on setting up Google
Cloud Platform or Ansible.

00:01:35.522 --> 00:01:37.230
DYLAN SILVA: OK, so
while that's running,

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.480
let's take a look at some
of the playbooks, shall we?

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:39.930
TOM MELENDEZ: Sure.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:40.770
Let me open it up.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:41.728
DYLAN SILVA: All right.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.140
So now that that's
open, it looks

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.210
like we have a playbook
that executes commands

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.490
from local hosts and
includes some variables

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.227
from various files
like last time.

00:01:55.227 --> 00:01:56.060
TOM MELENDEZ: Right.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.380
You can run this
command from anywhere,

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.150
as long as you have
the proper credentials.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.080
DYLAN SILVA: OK, cool.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.520
So first, we're creating a
couple of managed instance

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.020
groups.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.870
We set the name and zone, as
well as the starting size,

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.640
and we're also
setting the instance

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.789
template that should be used in
building these new instances.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:12.950
And then it looks like
the managed instance

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.940
group, also where you configure
the auto-scaling policy,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:16.720
is here as well.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:17.470
TOM MELENDEZ: Yes.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.939
DYLAN SILVA: OK, so that's
one of the greatest features

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:21.980
that I think are there
about scaling applications

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.695
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:23.695 --> 00:02:25.070
Can you tell us
a little bit more

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:26.875
about auto-scaling and depth?

00:02:26.875 --> 00:02:27.935
TOM MELENDEZ: OK.

00:02:27.935 --> 00:02:30.410
Well, with auto-scaling,
you can have

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.240
your host scale
up or down, based

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:33.980
on a policy you configure.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:37.370
The policy can be based on
things like CPU utilization

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.040
or what fraction of the
load balancer utilization

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.170
the instance group
should handle.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:45.210
You can even define policies
using custom metrics,

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.520
if those don't suit you.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.970
DYLAN SILVA: And details
are in documentation, right?

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.120
TOM MELENDEZ: Right.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.030
To keep it simple, we
went with CPU utilization.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:56.000
This policy basically
reads, "scale accordingly

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.110
to keep the CPU utilization
on the machines under 80%."

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.340
DYLAN SILVA: And that's it?

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:05.660
GCP will scale up those
instances for you?

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.220
TOM MELENDEZ: Yes,
to the max number

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.507
of instances that you specify.

00:03:08.507 --> 00:03:09.590
DYLAN SILVA: That's great.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:12.770
So I see we specified two
managed instance groups here

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:15.590
with basically the
same configuration.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:16.590
TOM MELENDEZ: Very true.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.440
We wanted to be
explicit here, but one

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.990
might consider using a
looping construct, like

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:22.307
with items or with [? depth. ?]

00:03:22.307 --> 00:03:24.890
DYLAN SILVA: And then finally,
for our managed instance group,

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.320
we have [? name and ?]
port specified.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.202
TOM MELENDEZ: Yes.

00:03:28.202 --> 00:03:29.660
Those are where
the backend service

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:30.800
will send the traffic to.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:31.850
DYLAN SILVA: Great segue.

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:33.860
So let's talk about
backend services.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.871
What is a backend service for?

00:03:35.871 --> 00:03:37.620
TOM MELENDEZ: Basically,
a backend service

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.320
is a collection of
interest groups.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.210
The backend service
lets the administrator

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:42.860
treat all of these
instance groups

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.980
as a single entity in terms
of serving capability.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.940
DYLAN SILVA: So what
do you mean by that?

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.314
TOM MELENDEZ: Well, we've
configured the managed instance

00:03:50.314 --> 00:03:53.220
groups to scale up
based on CPU previously.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.670
But really, we haven't
talked about how much traffic

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:56.840
we want them to handle.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.420
By grouping these instance
groups into a backend service,

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.410
We can set additional
configuration, as well as

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.630
a health check on
the whole entity.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:08.180
DYLAN SILVA: And then you can
have multiple backend services,

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:08.889
I imagine, right?

00:04:08.889 --> 00:04:09.721
TOM MELENDEZ: Right.

00:04:09.721 --> 00:04:12.240
This lets admins build out
their desired infrastructure,

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.930
placing groups of instances
where it makes sense for them,

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.180
and determining how much
traffic they should handle.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:20.006
DYLAN SILVA: Wow, this
really seems super powerful.

00:04:20.006 --> 00:04:20.839
TOM MELENDEZ: It is.

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:22.670
Another powerful and
customizable feature

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:25.662
is the URL map, which
we have up next.

00:04:25.662 --> 00:04:28.160
DYLAN SILVA: So now it looks
like all of the configuration

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.450
here is pretty minimal.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:31.070
It looks like just
giving it a name

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:34.250
and handling the backend
service that we created.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:36.365
What is URL map for anyways?

00:04:36.365 --> 00:04:37.490
TOM MELENDEZ: You're right.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:39.156
In this case, and for
many applications,

00:04:39.156 --> 00:04:42.440
a simple configuration,
such as this, is fine.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.500
In essence, the URL map maps
the URL to a backend service.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.050
We don't do it here, but you
can route different paths

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.020
to different backend services.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.090
DYLAN SILVA: So for
our configuration,

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.770
this playbook is
basically saying

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:58.130
map any URL to the default
backend service, right?

00:04:58.130 --> 00:04:59.090
TOM MELENDEZ: Right.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:02.000
Next is a target proxy, which
terminates the connection

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.180
from the client and routes
the request to a URL map.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.950
In this case, we're
using an HTTP proxy.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:12.060
DYLAN SILVA: So we could then
change that to an HTTPS proxy

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.094
and then still use the
same URL map though, right?

00:05:15.094 --> 00:05:16.010
TOM MELENDEZ: Correct.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:18.680
In that case, you would
also specify the SSL cert,

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:19.759
in addition to a URL map.

00:05:19.759 --> 00:05:21.800
DYLAN SILVA: And then
finally, create a static IP

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.187
address and a forwarding rule.

00:05:23.187 --> 00:05:24.020
TOM MELENDEZ: Right.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.311
Up until this point, we've
built out the load balancing

00:05:26.311 --> 00:05:27.290
capability.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.707
Now we're creating an IP address
and mapping to the proxy.

00:05:29.707 --> 00:05:30.540
DYLAN SILVA: Gotcha.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.660
So when a client makes
a request to this IP

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.091
address on that port, the
proxy's going to handle it.

00:05:35.091 --> 00:05:35.840
TOM MELENDEZ: Yep.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.240
And that's the entire
configuration for a global load

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:39.369
balanced web application.

00:05:39.369 --> 00:05:41.660
DYLAN SILVA: So cool-- not
a whole lot of configuration

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:42.280
then.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:43.954
How'd the provisioning go?

00:05:43.954 --> 00:05:46.120
TOM MELENDEZ: It was done
a lot sooner than we were.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.339
The load balancer was
created successfully.

00:05:48.339 --> 00:05:49.130
DYLAN SILVA: Great.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:50.990
So how does this look
in the web console?

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:52.400
TOM MELENDEZ: Let's take a look.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.870
Opening up the
dashboard, then we

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.090
click on Networking,
then Load Balancing.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:58.659
Let's click on the Advanced
menu down at the bottom here,

00:05:58.659 --> 00:06:00.950
which gives us the component
breakdown that mirrors how

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:03.140
we built that up in Ansible.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:05.330
We see the global
forwarding rule and the IP

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:06.790
address it maps to.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:11.330
Under Target Proxies, we see the
proxy name, the type of proxy,

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.680
and the URL map to apply.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.340
Clicking on Backend Services,
we see our backend service.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.440
Let's click on that.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:20.860
DYLAN SILVA: Hey,
cool-- traffic.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.620
So we can see how much
is being served out

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.097
of each instance group here.

00:06:24.097 --> 00:06:24.930
TOM MELENDEZ: Right.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:27.300
I generated this traffic
with a Bash one-liner using

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.620
[? cURL-- ?] one instance in our
east region, one in the west--

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.590
just to demonstrate the graphs.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.940
Interesting note that the
user can click right here

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.835
and adjust the
settings if they like.

00:06:37.835 --> 00:06:39.210
DYLAN SILVA: But
of course, those

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.585
are going to be overwritten
by the playbook the next time

00:06:41.585 --> 00:06:43.500
because of the desired
state of the system.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:44.760
TOM MELENDEZ: Yep, of course.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.010
Well, that's it-- another
quick in-and-out video

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.750
showing Ansible and GCP.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:49.445
Thanks, Dylan.

00:06:49.445 --> 00:06:50.820
DYLAN SILVA: You're
welcome, Tom.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:52.320
Hey, it's been great
showing viewers

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.615
how to use Ansible with
Google Cloud Platform.

00:06:54.615 --> 00:06:55.740
Are you up for another one?

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:56.940
TOM MELENDEZ: I sure am.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.898
DYLAN SILVA: We've got
